  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows an upper impulse lifting across \nwestern Nebraska with another piece of energy approaching southwest \nTX. At the surface, strong cold front stretches from eastern \nNebraska into north central KS with sfc low over nw KS. \n\nWe are expecting a gradual increase in showers from the southwest \ntonight as upper impulse lifts across TX and into OK with south \ncentral KS seeing the better rain coverage. For Tue, the higher \nshower chances will be in the morning with quick decrease for the \nafternoon hours as we get into some subsidence as the impulse moves \ninto the Ozark region. Did go ahead and insert some slight pops for \nlate Tue night into early Wed morning southeast of the KS Turnpike \nas low level jet develops and 850-700mb moisture transport ramps-up. \n\nAttention will then turn to another southern stream impulse set to \nlift out of southwest TX on Thu and into southern OK Thu night. This \nwill spread fairly high rainfall chances across most of the area \nstarting Fri and continuing through Fri night into Sat morning. \nWhile much of the region will likely get wet, not looking for much \nin the way of heavy rain or flooding.\n\nAs far as temps go, confidence remains high that above normal \nreadings will continue. Normal highs for this time of year are \nin the mid 60s and we will be looking at highs in the upper 60s \nand 70s through most of the forecast. Wed and Thu look like the \nwarmest days, with many areas reaching 80 degrees.",
